<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Olmsted is a 73-year-old man with mild dementia of undetermined type who returns with his caregiver for his subsequent annual wellness visit, six weeks after his three-month dementia review. He answered the health risk assessment himself, with his caregiver reading the questions and adding what he could not recall. His cold in July cleared in two weeks, and he has had no further night-time confusion since the antihistamine was stopped. He takes donepezil 10 mg each morning, given by his caregiver from a weekly organizer, with no nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, or vivid dreams. He no longer drives. He walks most days with company and goes to an adult day program twice a week, which he enjoys for the gardens. He has had no falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays this year. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Health risk assessment answered by the patient with caregiver help because of dementia</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays this year</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Donepezil 10 mg daily from a weekly organizer; no over-the-counter medicines since the cold</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Mild dementia of undetermined type, CDR 1</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in basic activities of daily living; needs help with finances, medicines, and travel</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How completed</td><td>Answered by the patient with his caregiver reading the questions and adding what he could not recall</td></tr><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good, in his words</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives at home with family; adult day program twice a week; misses his work but enjoys the gardens and visitors</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never used tobacco</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>None since the first visit (AUDIT-C 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks 30 to 45 minutes most days with company</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Three regular meals; weight stable over the past three months</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Sleeps through most nights; no sleep medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, toileting, and eating</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Caregiver manages medicines, bill paying, and transport, with his agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>No firearms in the home; medicines kept locked; night lights in place; smoke detectors working</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>No falls this year</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>No hearing concern; reading glasses; no eye examination in the past two years</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Known mild dementia; his caregiver notices no change since the spring</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

